"What is Wizard mode, and how do I use it?"

     The game maintainer can use wizard-mode as a debugging aid.  Try:
     "nethack -u wizard -D" or "nethack -uWizard -D"

"I've undergone a Name Change and then saved the game.  How do I restore
the game?"

     In most implementations you have to tell the game your new name, as
     in: "nethack -u <newname>"

&&&  "I'm stuck in [level whatever] -- where do I go next?"

     With the possible exception of the bottom level of the Rogue's quest,
     there is always a way to go SOMEwhere, whether it be up, down, or
     across dimensions somehow.  Just remember that there are more ways
     to cross levels than just stairs -- trapdoors, magic portals, level teleport
     traps, artifacts, and quest mentors can all get you to other levels, too.

     Note about Magic Portals:  
        Regardless of how it may seem at times, Magic Portals do NOT just vanish!  
        Any given portal has two ends, and these ends do not move -- though they 
        are not visible until searched for, revealed magically, or stepped onto.  
        If you're having trouble getting out of a quest or a tower, then you need to 
        search more systematically, my friend ;) because if you arrived through a 
        portal, then the way out is on the very same square where you first 
        appeared.

&&&  "Where is Fort Ludios?"

     (There has been more inaccurate posting in r.g.h about Fort Ludios recently 
     than about any other subject I've ever seen, except perhaps for Street Fighter 
     or Mortal Kombat in r.g.v.a ;)

     Fort Ludios is a rather auriferous little castle that lies on the other
     side of a magic portal.  Fort Ludios does NOT exist in each and every NetHack
     game, but when the NetHack DOES generate Ludios in any given game, the
     portal that sends one there will be inside a 'vault' (or, as the Oracle says, the 
     'Magic Memory Vault') somewhere before the Castle's level.  I do not know the 
     minimum level that the Ludios portal can appear at, but from personal 
     experience, I know that it can appear from about level 8 on down to the level
     preceding the castle (about 29 at the deepest).

     If you don't know what a 'vault' is, then play archaeologists and tourists a bit,
     and explore those levels where you hear 'someone searching,' or 'someone 
     counting gold,' or perhaps 'the footsteps of a guard on patrol.'

"The Dungeon collapsed.  Can I recover this game?"

     The official binaries contain recover programs that can put level files
     back together into a saved game; if you are playing on a UNIX platform,
     you will probably need the superuser's help to recover the game.

"Where can I get <popular hack of NetHack>?" 

     There are several popular, independent hacks of the official NetHack
     source code around, and their availability (and version compatibility)
     varies by their creators. 

   o 'NetHack --' 

     NetHack -- is a revision of NetHack 3.1.3 that includes some unusual 
     monsters and items. 


   o 'Chrhack' 

    ChrHack (v 2.3) is a front end for IBM pc that replaces the NetHack
    text graphics with bitmaps. The program does not alter the NetHack 
    executable in any way. 

    The site and path for the file is:
       ftp.ulowell.edu:/msdos/Games/Utils/chrhac23.zip  

    Please direct inquiries to the author, Mike Marcelais 
    (mrmarcel@eos.ncsu.edu). 

